    "evidence": "Root cause / sink: FilenameUtils.doNormalize(String, char, boolean) at src/main/java/org/apache/commons/io/FilenameUtils.java:348-442, driven by FilenameUtils.getPrefixLength(String) at lines 633-687. Trust boundary: any untrusted, externally-supplied path string that a caller passes into FilenameUtils.normalize()/normalizeNoEndSeparator() expecting it to either fully resolve \"..\" segments or return null when the path would escape above its root (this is the method's documented contract, verified interactively: normalize(\"/../secret.txt\") -> null, normalize(\"//../secret.txt\") -> \"//../secret.txt\" unchanged, on the unpatched tree).\n\nConcretely: getPrefixLength() treats any path beginning with two separators (lines 674-682) as a Windows UNC prefix \"\\\\\\\\server\\\\\", computing the prefix length as `indexOf(separator, 2) + 1` with no validation of the \"server name\" substring. When that substring is literally \"..\" (e.g. \"//../foo\"), the prefix absorbs the traversal token entirely (prefix=5 for \"//../foo\"). doNormalize's double-dot-collapse loop (lines 405-430) only begins scanning at `prefix + 2`, so the \"..\" that was swallowed into the prefix is never evaluated by the \"no parent segment -> return null\" logic that correctly fires for \"/../foo\" (prefix=1, dot-dot loop starts at 3, immediately trips the `i == prefix + 2` early-return-null branch at line 409-411). The result is silently passed through unresolved and non-null.\n\nEvery trace in the alert is downstream of this same defect, and a complete fix must guard ALL of them:\n1. FilenameUtils.doNormalize/getPrefixLength itself (root cause) \u2014 the direct sink exercised by this PoV via FilenameUtils.normalize(String).\n2. FilenameUtils.normalize(String, boolean) (line 241-244) and normalizeNoEndSeparator(String) / normalizeNoEndSeparator(String, boolean) (lines 288-337) \u2014 all route through the same doNormalize/getPrefixLength pair and inherit the identical bypass.\n3. FileSystemUtils.freeSpaceUnix / freeSpaceWindows / freeSpaceOS (traces at lines 147-148, 178-179, 209-210, 274, 282) \u2014 call FilenameUtils.normalize(path) (line 304) on the caller-supplied `path` argument and then splice the (possibly mis-normalized, still-traversal-bearing) result directly into a `cmdAttribs` String[] (lines 313/316) that is handed to `openProcess`/`Runtime.exec` (lines 398-573) as a literal argument to `df`/`dir` \u2014 i.e. the same normalize() defect here feeds a command argument rather than a file path, so a value normalize() failed to null-out or fully resolve can carry an attacker-influenced path fragment into the spawned process's arguments.",
