    "regression_hint": "Invariant a correct fix must enforce: FilenameUtils.getPrefixLength()/doNormalize() must treat a path whose \"double-separator prefix\" host/server-name component is \".\" or \"..\" as invalid (matching upstream commons-io's CVE-2021-29425 fix), so that FilenameUtils.normalize(\"//../x\") returns null, exactly as FilenameUtils.normalize(\"/../x\") already does \u2014 i.e. normalize() must be consistent regardless of how many leading separators precede a leading \"..\" with no real parent segment to consume.\n\nKnown bypass variants / sibling call sites the fix must also verify (not just the one path this PoV exercises):\n- Backslash form: \"\\\\\\\\..\\\\secret.txt\" (Windows separator variant of the same UNC-prefix confusion).\n- Mixed separators: \"//..\\\\secret.txt\", \"\\\\\\\\../secret.txt\".\n- Trailing content after the swallowed \"..\": \"//../../secret.txt\" currently returns null already (second \"..\" trips the early check) but should be re-tested after the fix to ensure no regression.\n- Deeper nesting: \"//../a/../../secret.txt\" and other combinations that still rely on the same swallowed leading \"..\".\n- normalizeNoEndSeparator(String) and normalizeNoEndSeparator(String, boolean) \u2014 same defect, separate public entry points, must be covered by the same fix in getPrefixLength since both delegate to doNormalize.\n- FileSystemUtils.freeSpaceUnix/freeSpaceWindows/freeSpaceOS \u2014 once normalize() is fixed to return null for these payloads, confirm the callers actually reject a null return (i.e. they don't silently pass a null/garbage value into the cmdAttribs array and invoke the OS command anyway); this is a secondary check beyond the normalize() fix itself since the alert traces show the tainted `path` flowing all the way to cmdAttribs/openProcess.",
